<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-9-1-DegreesIsomorphism" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Degrees, Isomorphism</title>

<p>
    A graph is an abstract model of a network. We are surrounded by networks:
    social, communication, biological and their study is of interest in many
    realms, both theoretical and applied. We start with some examples.
  </p>

  <example>
    <title>Kevin Bacon network</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Kevin Bacon is a famous American actor that has appeared in many movies.
        The Bacon graph or the collaboration graph of actors is the network
        whose nodes/points are movie actors, where two actors are connected by
        an edge (a segment or curve joining the corresponding points) if they
        have been in a movie together.
      </p>

  <p>
    
Insert figure " The actors network on Kevin Bacon, Cate Blanchett, Dustin Hoffman,
          Brad Pitt and Meryl Streep, Emma Watson and Bruce Willis"
      </p>

      <p>
        The whole network is much larger and evolves in time as more movies are
        being made. This network has also led to the notion of the Bacon number
        which is defined as follows.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Kevin Bacon is the only person with Bacon number <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Any actor that has appeared in a movie with Kevin Bacon gets Bacon
              number <m>1</m>. All the actors in Figure ??
              except Kevin Bacon, have Bacon number <m>1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Any actor that has does not have Bacon number <m>1</m> and has
              appeared in a movie with an actor of Bacon number <m>1</m>, gets
              Bacon number <m>2</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              For <m>j\geq 3</m>, an actor that does not have Bacon number
              <m>j-1</m> or less, will get Bacon number <m>j</m>, if that actor
              has appeared in a movie with an actor of Bacon number <m>j-1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        If an actor cannot be connected to Kevin Bacon as above via a chain of
        common movies, then its Bacon number is <m>+\infty</m>. Each of the
        authors of this book has Bacon number <m>+\infty</m>, for example.
      </p>

      <p>
        While there are many people with Bacon number <m>+\infty</m>, it appears
        that the largest (finite) Bacon number is <m>10</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>


<example>
    <title>Erdős collaboration network</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Paul Erdős (1913-1996) was a famous Hungarian mathematician who wrote
        <m>1447</m> research papers and had <m>512</m> co-authors
        <fn>These numbers are obtained from <url href="www.ams.org/mathscinet">AMS MathSciNet</url>. There are slightly different numbers at the Erdős Number Project.</fn>.
        Erdős's work has greatly influenced many areas of mathematics,
        especially discrete mathematics.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider the following collaboration network. The members of this
        network are mathematicians or authors of mathematical papers. Two
        mathematicians are joined by an edge in this network if they are
        co-authors on a mathematical paper or book. Of course, this network
        evolves with time as more papers and books are being published and more
        co-authorship relations are being created. To avoid amiguities, we focus
        on the collaboration network at present time.
      </p>

     
        <p>
          INSERT FIGURE: The collaboration network of Erdös, Chung, Einstein, Graham and Straus
        </p>
  

      <p>
        In this network, Erdős is joined by an edge to Fan Chung, Ron Graham and
        Ernst Straus, but is not joined by an edge to Albert Einstein. Ernst
        Straus who wrote a paper with Einstein and wrote a paper with Ron
        Graham, but did not write a paper with with Fan Chung. The collaboration
        network with these five people is in Figure ??
      </p>

      <p>
        Based on the large collaboration network or graph of mathematicians, the
        notion of the Erdős number has been introduced
        <fn>The first reference seems to be the paper ???. And what is your Erdős number? by Casper Goffman in The American Mathematical Monthly in 1969.</fn>.
        Each mathematician receives a number which (we will define these terms
        later) is the shortest length of a path from that mathematician to Erdős
        in the collaboration graph. For now, we can define these numbers as
        follows.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Erdős is the only person that has Erdős number <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Every co-author of Erdős gets number <m>1</m>. Therefore, Chung,
              Graham and Straus have Erdős number <m>1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Each person that does not have Erdős number <m>1</m>, but has
              written a paper with someone of Erdős number <m>1</m>, gets Erdős
              number <m>2</m>. Einstein has Erdős number <m>2</m> via Straus.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Each person that does not have Erdős number <m>0,1,2</m>, but has
              written a paper with someone of Erdős number <m>2</m>, receives
              Erdős number <m>3</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The procedure continues in the same manner similar to the
              assignment of Bacon numbers earlier. For <m>j\geq 3</m>, a person
              that does not have Erdős number <m>j-1</m> or less, gets Erdős
              number <m>j</m> if that person has written a paper with someone
              with Erdős number <m>j-1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        Currently, the largest Erdős number is <m>13</m>. Every person that does
        not have a (finite) Erdős number, gets Erdős number <m>+\infty</m>. The
        authors of this book have the following Erdős numbers:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Novi Bong has Erdős number <m>2</m> via Ortrud Oellerman.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Sebastian Cioabă has Erdős number <m>2</m> via Noga Alon or Peter
              Cameron or Brendan McKay or Ram Murty.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Robert Coulter has Erdős number <m>3</m> via Aart Blokhuis and
              Peter Cameron.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example>
    <title>An abstract social network/graph</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        We want to describe the friendship relations between a finite set of
        people.
      </p>

   
        <p> INSERT FIGURE: Another graph/network example
      </p>

      <p>
        Assume that we have five people which we represent in Figure
        ?? as dots or points in the plane:
        <md xml:id="eq-Vnet0" number="yes">
          x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4},x_{5}.
        </md>
        In Figure ??, we draw a segment joining two
        points if the two persons represented by these points are friends. We
        can represent these segments as <m>2</m>-subsets of the
        <m>\{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4},x_{5}\}</m>:
        <md xml:id="eq-Enet0" number="yes">
          \{x_{1},x_{2}\}, \{x_{1},x_{4}\}, \{x_{1},x_{5}\}, \{x_{2},x_{3}\}, \{x_{2},x_{4}\}, \{x_{4},x_{5}\}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
</section>